<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eliezer Ben-Yehuda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Eliezer Yitzhak Perlman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eliezer Ben-Yehuda was a Jewish linguist, lexicographer and journalist and the primary driving force behind the revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> language. Born Eliezer Yitzhak Perlman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in 1881 joined the First Aliyah and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He raised his son Ben-Zion entirely in Hebrew, making him the first native speaker of Hebrew in modern times, and edited the newspaper HaZvi. A major figure in founding the Committee of the Hebrew Language, he began the first modern Hebrew dictionary, completed after his death by his wife Hemda. He died of tuberculosis in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eliezer-ben-yehuda.jpg" alt="Eliezer Ben-Yehuda" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ya'ackov Ben-Dov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Eliezer_Ben-Yehuda_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eliezer Yitzhak Perlman was born on 7 January 1858 in Luzhki, in the Vilna Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to Yehuda Leib and Tzipora Perlman, who were Chabad hasidim. His native language was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> from the age of three at a cheder, and by twelve had read large parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, Mishna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>. His family hoped he would become a rabbi, and he was sent to a yeshiva, where he met the Hebrew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haskalah">Jewish Enlightenment</a>. After graduating in 1877 he studied for four years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, including at the Sorbonne. A conversation there with a Jew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> who spoke Hebrew convinced him that Hebrew could be revived as the language of a nation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1881 Ben-Yehuda joined the First Aliyah and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, where he taught at a school of the Alliance Israélite Universelle. He sought a language that could replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a> and regional dialects in daily life, and he wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> could live only if the nation was revived and returned to its fatherland. Before this, Hebrew had remained in continuous use in liturgy, study and writing. To supply missing terms he coined words from Semitic roots, and some became part of the language while others never caught on. He raised his son Ben-Zion entirely in Hebrew, making him the first native speaker of Hebrew in modern times.</p><p>He edited the newspapers HaZvi and Hashkafa. HaZvi was closed for a year after opposition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'s ultra-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> community, which objected to using the holy tongue for everyday speech. In December 1893 he and his father-in-law were imprisoned by the Ottoman authorities after accusations that they were inciting rebellion. He was a major figure in founding the Committee of the Hebrew Language, later the Academy of the Hebrew Language, and he began the first modern Hebrew dictionary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ben-Yehuda's first wife, Devora, died of tuberculosis in 1891, and three of his children died of diphtheria soon afterwards. He then married her younger sister, who took the name Hemda. He died of tuberculosis in December 1922, aged 64, and was buried on the Mount of Olives; 30,000 people attended his funeral. Hemda, a journalist and author, secured the completion of the dictionary in the following decades. The historian Cecil Roth is quoted as saying that before Ben-Yehuda Jews could speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and after him they did.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eliezer Ben-Yehuda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ben-Zion <b>Ben-Yehuda</b>, born in Jerusalem in 1882 and raised by his father Eliezer to speak only Hebrew, is often called the first native speaker of the language in modern times.</blockquote><a href="/language/hebrew">Hebrew · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahad Ha’am (Asher Ginsberg) founded cultural Zionism, seeking a spiritual centre in Palestine and opposing political solutions that victimised non-Jews, and <b>Eliezer Ben-Yehuda</b> is most closely associated with the creation of Modern Hebrew.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Eliezer Ben-Yehuda</b> settled in Jerusalem in 1881, insisted on speaking only Hebrew at home and compiled a great dictionary; his son Ben-Zion, born in 1882, is often called the first native speaker of modern times.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-written-word">The Written Word</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lužki, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. Died in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.35,27.87&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Lužki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Eliezer Ben-Yehuda"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4896 5396h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4896 5396h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.35,27.87&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
